             "categories" -> "astro-ph", "license" -> Null, "abstract" -> 
             "  We investigate the effect of a strong large scale magnetic \
field on the\nreflection of high frequency acoustic modes in rapidly \
oscillating Ap stars. To\nthat end we consider a toy model composed of an \
isothermal atmosphere matched\nonto a polytropic interior and determine the \
numerical solution to the set of\nideal magneto-hydrodynamic equations in a \
local plane-parallel approximation\nwith constant gravity. Using the \
numerical solution in combination with\napproximate analytical solutions that \
are valid in the limits where the\nmagnetic and acoustic components are \
decoupled, we calculate the relative\nfraction of energy flux that is carried \
away in each oscillation cycle by\nrunning acoustic waves in the atmosphere \
and running magnetic waves in the\ninterior. For oscillation frequencies \
above the acoustic cutoff we show that\nmost energy losses associated with \
the presence of running waves occur in\nregions where the magnetic field is \
close to vertical. Moreover, by considering\nthe depth dependence of the \
energy associated with the magnetic component of\nthe wave in the atmosphere \
we show that a fraction of the wave energy is kept\nin the oscillation every \
cycle. For frequencies above the acoustic cutoff\nfrequency such energy is \
concentrated in regions where the magnetic field is\nsignificantly inclined \
in relation to the local vertical. Even though our\ncalculations were aimed \
at studying oscillations with frequencies above the\nacoustic cutoff \
frequency, based on our results we discuss what results may be\nexpected for \
oscillations of lower frequency.\n", 
