                RowBox[{
                 "\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Laura Pentericci\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"L.Pentericci, W. Van Reeven, C.L. Carilli, H.J.A. \
Rottgering and G.K.\\n  Miley\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"VLA radio continuum observations of a new sample of high \
redshift radio\\n  galaxies\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Accepted for publication in A&A Supplement\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Astron.Astrophys.Suppl.Ser.145:121-159,2000\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1051/aas:2000104\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"astro-ph\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  We present new deep multi-frequency radio-polarimetric \
images of a sample of\\nhigh redshift radio galaxies (HzRGs), having redshift \
between 1.7 and 4.1. The\\nradio data at 4.7 and 8.2 GHz were taken with the \
Very Large Array in the A\\nconfiguration and provide a highest angular \
resolution of 0.2''. Maps of total\\nintensity, radio spectral index, radio \
polarization and internal magnetic field\\nare presented for each source. The \
morphology of most objects is that of\\nstandard FRII double radio sources, \
but several contain multiple hot-spots in\\none or both lobes. Compared to \
similar samples of HzRGs previously imaged,\\nthere is a higher fraction \
(29%) of compact steep spectrum sources (i.e.\\nsources with a projected \
linear size less than 20 kpc). Radio cores are\\nidentified in about half of \
the sample and tend to have relatively steep\\nspectra (alpha < -1). \
Polarization is detected in all but 4 sources, with\\ntypical polarization at \
8.2 GHz of around 10-20%. The Faraday rotation can be\\nmeasured in most of \
the radio galaxies: the observed rotation measure (RM) of 8\\nradio sources \
exceeds 100 rad m^{-2} in at least one of the lobes, with large\\ngradients \
between the two lobes. We find no dependence of Faraday rotation with\\nother \
properties of the radio sources. If the origin of the Faraday rotation \
is\\nlocal to the sources, as we believe, then the intrinsic RM is more than \
a 1000\\nrad m^{-2}. Because low redshift radio galaxies residing at the \
center of\\nclusters usually show extreme RMs, we suggest that the high-z \
large RM sources\\nalso lie in very dense environments. Finally, we find that \
the fraction of\\npowerful radio galaxies with extreme Faraday rotation \
increases with redshift,\\nas would be expected if their average environment \
tends to become denser with\\ndecreasing cosmic epoch.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
