and internal magnetic field\nare presented for each source. The morphology of \
most objects is that of\nstandard FRII double radio sources, but several \
contain multiple hot-spots in\none or both lobes. Compared to similar samples \
of HzRGs previously imaged,\nthere is a higher fraction (29%) of compact \
steep spectrum sources (i.e.\nsources with a projected linear size less than \
20 kpc). Radio cores are\nidentified in about half of the sample and tend to \
have relatively steep\nspectra (alpha < -1). Polarization is detected in all \
but 4 sources, with\ntypical polarization at 8.2 GHz of around 10-20%. The \
Faraday rotation can be\nmeasured in most of the radio galaxies: the observed \
rotation measure (RM) of 8\nradio sources exceeds 100 rad m^{-2} in at least \
one of the lobes, with large\ngradients between the two lobes. We find no \
dependence of Faraday rotation with\nother properties of the radio sources. \
If the origin of the Faraday rotation is\nlocal to the sources, as we \
believe, then the intrinsic RM is more than a 1000\nrad m^{-2}. Because low \
redshift radio galaxies residing at the center of\nclusters usually show \
extreme RMs, we suggest that the high-z large RM sources\nalso lie in very \
dense environments. Finally, we find that the fraction of\npowerful radio \
galaxies with extreme Faraday rotation increases with redshift,\nas would be \
expected if their average environment tends to become denser with\ndecreasing \
cosmic epoch.\n", 
