                RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"astro-ph\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  
                  "\"  Among a number of gamma-ray bursts whose host galaxies \
are known, GRB971214\\nstands out for its high redshift(z=3.25) and the Lya \
emission line having a\\nP-Cygni type profile. which is interpreted to be a \
direct consequence of the\\nexpanding supershell. From a simple Voigt-fitting \
analysis, we estimate the\\nexpansion velocity of the supershell and the \
neutral column density. The theory\\non the evolution of supernova remnants \
is used to propose that the supershell\\nis at the adiabatic phase, with its \
radius $R=10E^{1/2}_{53}$pc, its age\\nt=4.7\\\\times 10^3 E^{1/2}_{53} yrs, \
and the density of the ambient medium\\n$n_1=5.4E^{-1/2}_{53} cm^{-3}$, where \
$E_{53}=E/10^{53}$ ergs. I estimate the\\nkinetic energy of the supershell to \
be $E_k=7.3\\\\times 10^{52} E_{53}$ ergs. I\\nprovide an observational \
evidence that the gamma-ray burst can occur in a giant\\nH II region whose \
environment is similar to that in star-forming galaxies.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
