                  "\"Peter J. Brown, Luc Dessart, Stephen T. Holland, Stefan \
Immler, Wayne\\n  Landsman, Stephane Blondin, Alexander J. Blustin, Alice \
Breeveld, Gulab C.\\n  Dewangan, Neil Gehrels, Robert B. Hutchins, Robert P. \
Kirshner, Keith O.\\n  Mason, Paolo A. Mazzali, Peter Milne, Maryam Modjaz, \
and Peter W. A. Roming\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Early Ultraviolet, Optical and X-Ray Observations of the \
Type IIP SN\\n  2005cs in M51 with Swift\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"15 pages, 6 figures. Accepted for publication by \
Astrophysical\\n  Journal\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Astrophys.J.659:1488-1495,2007\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1086/511968\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"astro-ph\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"license\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  We report early photospheric-phase observations of the \
Type IIP Supernova\\n(SN) 2005cs obtained by Swift's Ultraviolet-Optical and \
X-Ray Telescopes.\\nObservations started within two days of discovery and \
continued regularly for\\nthree weeks. During this time the V-band magnitude \
remained essentially\\nconstant, while the UV was initially bright but \
steadily faded until below the\\nbrightness of an underlying UV-bright HII \
region. This UV decay is similar to\\nSNe II observed by the International \
Ultraviolet Explorer. UV grism spectra\\nshow the P-Cygni absorption of MgII \
2798A, indicating a photospheric origin of\\nthe UV flux. Based on non-LTE \
model atmosphere calculations with the CMFGEN\\ncode, we associate the rapid \
evolution of the UV flux with the cooling of the\\nejecta, the peak of the \
spectral energy distribution (SED) shifting from ~700A\\non June 30th to \
~1200A on July 5th. Furthermore, the corresponding\\nrecombination of the \
ejecta, e.g., the transition from FeIII to FeII, induces\\nconsiderable \
strengthening of metal line-blanketing at and above the\\nphotosphere, \
blocking more effectively this fading UV flux. SN2005cs was not\\ndetected in \
X-rays, and the upper limit to the X-ray luminosity yields a limit\\nto the \
mass loss rate of the progenitor of about 10^-5 solar masses per \
year.\\nOverall, Swift presents a unique opportunity to capture the early and \
fast\\nevolution of Type II SNe in the UV, providing additional constraints \
on the\\nreddening, the SED shortward of 4000A, and the ionization state and \
temperature\\nof the photon-decoupling regions.\\n\""}]}, {
