             "  We report early photospheric-phase observations of the Type \
IIP Supernova\n(SN) 2005cs obtained by Swift's Ultraviolet-Optical and X-Ray \
Telescopes.\nObservations started within two days of discovery and continued \
regularly for\nthree weeks. During this time the V-band magnitude remained \
essentially\nconstant, while the UV was initially bright but steadily faded \
until below the\nbrightness of an underlying UV-bright HII region. This UV \
decay is similar to\nSNe II observed by the International Ultraviolet \
Explorer. UV grism spectra\nshow the P-Cygni absorption of MgII 2798A, \
indicating a photospheric origin of\nthe UV flux. Based on non-LTE model \
atmosphere calculations with the CMFGEN\ncode, we associate the rapid \
evolution of the UV flux with the cooling of the\nejecta, the peak of the \
spectral energy distribution (SED) shifting from ~700A\non June 30th to \
~1200A on July 5th. Furthermore, the corresponding\nrecombination of the \
ejecta, e.g., the transition from FeIII to FeII, induces\nconsiderable \
strengthening of metal line-blanketing at and above the\nphotosphere, \
blocking more effectively this fading UV flux. SN2005cs was not\ndetected in \
X-rays, and the upper limit to the X-ray luminosity yields a limit\nto the \
mass loss rate of the progenitor of about 10^-5 solar masses per year.\n\
Overall, Swift presents a unique opportunity to capture the early and fast\n\
evolution of Type II SNe in the UV, providing additional constraints on the\n\
reddening, the SED shortward of 4000A, and the ionization state and \
temperature\nof the photon-decoupling regions.\n", 
