             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  Recent advances in nanofabrication methods have made it \
possible to create\ncomplex two-dimensional artificial structures, such as \
fractals, where\nelectrons can be confined. The optoelectronic and plasmonic \
properties of these\nexotic quantum electron systems are largely unexplored. \
In this article, we\ncalculate the optical conductivity of a two-dimensional \
electron gas in a\nSierpinski carpet (SC). The SC is a paradigmatic fractal \
that can be fabricated\nin a planar solid-state matrix by means of an \
iterative procedure. We show that\nthe optical conductivity as a function of \
frequency (i.e. the optical spectrum)\nconverges, at finite temperature, as a \
function of the fractal iteration. The\ncalculated optical spectrum features \
sharp peaks at frequencies determined by\nthe smallest geometric details at a \
given fractal iteration. Each peak is due\nto excitations within sets of \
electronic state-pairs, whose wave functions are\ncharacterized by quantum \
confinement in the SC at specific length scales,\nrelated to the frequency of \
the peak.\n", 
