             "Mott transitions of exciton-polaritons and indirect excitons in \
a\n  periodic potential", "comments" -> "30 pages, 8 figures", "journal-ref" -> 
             "Phys. Rev. B 81, 205312 (2010)", "doi" -> 
             "10.1103/PhysRevB.81.205312", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "cond-mat.mes-hall cond-mat.other", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  We derive an effective Bose-Hubbard model that predicts a \
phase transition\nfrom Bose-Einstein condensate to Mott insulator in two \
different systems\nsubject to applied periodic potentials: microcavity \
exciton-polaritons and\nindirect excitons. Starting from a microscopic \
Hamiltonian of electrons and\nholes, we derive an effective Bose-Hubbard \
model for both systems and evaluate\nthe on-site Coulomb interaction U and \
hopping transition amplitudes t.\nExperimental parameters required for \
observing a phase transition between a\nBose-Einstein condensate and a Mott \
insulator are discussed. Our results\nsuggest that strong periodic potentials \
and polaritons with a very large\nexcitonic component are required for \
observing the phase transition. The form\nof the indirect exciton interaction \
is derived including direct and exchange\ncomponents of the Coulomb \
interaction. For indirect excitons, the system\ncrosses over from a \
Bose-Hubbard model into a double layer Fermi-Hubbard model\nas a function of \
increasing bilayer separation. The Fermi-Hubbard model\nparameters are \
calculated, and the criteria for the location of this crossover\nare derived. \
We conjecture that a crossover between a Bose Mott insulator to a\nFermi Mott \
insulator should occur with increasing bilayer separation.\n", 
