                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Nanoscale thermal imaging of dissipation in quantum \
systems\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Submitted to Nature\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Nature 539, 407-410 (2016)\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1038/nature19843\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"cond-mat.mes-hall cond-mat.mtrl-sci cond-mat.supr-con \
physics.ins-det\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  Energy dissipation is a fundamental process governing \
the dynamics of\\nphysical, chemical, and biological systems. It is also one \
of the main\\ncharacteristics distinguishing quantum and classical phenomena. \
In condensed\\nmatter physics, in particular, scattering mechanisms, loss of \
quantum\\ninformation, or breakdown of topological protection are deeply \
rooted in the\\nintricate details of how and where the dissipation occurs. \
Despite its vital\\nimportance the microscopic behavior of a system is \
usually not formulated in\\nterms of dissipation because the latter is not a \
readily measureable quantity\\non the microscale. Although nanoscale \
thermometry is gaining much recent\\ninterest, the existing thermal imaging \
methods lack the necessary sensitivity\\nand are unsuitable for low \
temperature operation required for study of quantum\\nsystems. Here we report \
a superconducting quantum interference nano-thermometer\\ndevice with sub 50 \
nm diameter that resides at the apex of a sharp pipette and\\nprovides \
scanning cryogenic thermal sensing with four orders of magnitude\\nimproved \
thermal sensitivity of below 1 {\\\\mu}K/Hz1/2. The \
non-contact\\nnon-invasive thermometry allows thermal imaging of very low \
nanoscale energy\\ndissipation down to the fundamental Landauer limit of 40 \
fW for continuous\\nreadout of a single qubit at 1 GHz at 4.2 K. These \
advances enable observation\\nof dissipation due to single electron charging \
of individual quantum dots in\\ncarbon nanotubes and reveal a novel \
dissipation mechanism due to resonant\\nlocalized states in hBN encapsulated \
graphene, opening the door to direct\\nimaging of nanoscale dissipation \
processes in quantum matter.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
                    RowBox[{
                    "\"created\"", "\[Rule]", 
