          Annotation[#, 
           
           TableForm[{
            "id" -> "1609.01487", "submitter" -> "Dorri Halbertal", "authors" -> 
             "Dorri Halbertal, Jo Cuppens, Moshe Ben Shalom, Lior Embon, \
Nitzan\n  Shadmi, Yonathan Anahory, HR Naren, Jayanta Sarkar, Aviram Uri, \
Yuval Ronen,\n  Yury Myasoedov, Leonid Levitov, Ernesto Joselevich, Andre \
Konstantin Geim and\n  Eli Zeldov", "title" -> 
             "Nanoscale thermal imaging of dissipation in quantum systems", 
             "comments" -> "Submitted to Nature", "journal-ref" -> 
             "Nature 539, 407-410 (2016)", "doi" -> "10.1038/nature19843", 
             "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "cond-mat.mes-hall cond-mat.mtrl-sci cond-mat.supr-con \
physics.ins-det", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  Energy dissipation is a fundamental process governing the \
dynamics of\nphysical, chemical, and biological systems. It is also one of \
the main\ncharacteristics distinguishing quantum and classical phenomena. In \
condensed\nmatter physics, in particular, scattering mechanisms, loss of \
quantum\ninformation, or breakdown of topological protection are deeply \
rooted in the\nintricate details of how and where the dissipation occurs. \
Despite its vital\nimportance the microscopic behavior of a system is usually \
not formulated in\nterms of dissipation because the latter is not a readily \
measureable quantity\non the microscale. Although nanoscale thermometry is \
gaining much recent\ninterest, the existing thermal imaging methods lack the \
necessary sensitivity\nand are unsuitable for low temperature operation \
required for study of quantum\nsystems. Here we report a superconducting \
quantum interference nano-thermometer\ndevice with sub 50 nm diameter that \
resides at the apex of a sharp pipette and\nprovides scanning cryogenic \
thermal sensing with four orders of magnitude\nimproved thermal sensitivity \
of below 1 {\\mu}K/Hz1/2. The non-contact\nnon-invasive thermometry allows \
thermal imaging of very low nanoscale energy\ndissipation down to the \
fundamental Landauer limit of 40 fW for continuous\nreadout of a single qubit \
at 1 GHz at 4.2 K. These advances enable observation\nof dissipation due to \
single electron charging of individual quantum dots in\ncarbon nanotubes and \
reveal a novel dissipation mechanism due to resonant\nlocalized states in hBN \
encapsulated graphene, opening the door to direct\nimaging of nanoscale \
dissipation processes in quantum matter.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
                "Tue, 6 Sep 2016 11:14:30 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
