                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  Topologically stable matters can have a long lifetime, \
even if\\nthermodynamically costly, when the thermal agitation is \
sufficiently low. A\\nmagnetic skyrmion lattice (SkL) represents a unique \
form of long-range magnetic\\norder that is topologically stable, and \
therefore, a long-lived, metastable SkL\\ncan form. Experimental observations \
of the SkL in bulk crystals, however, have\\nmostly been limited to a finite \
and narrow temperature region in which the SkL\\nis thermodynamically stable; \
thus, the benefits of the topological stability\\nremain unclear. Here, we \
report a metastable SkL created by quenching a\\nthermodynamically stable \
SkL. Hall-resistivity measurements of MnSi reveal\\nthat, although the \
metastable SkL is short-lived at high temperatures, the\\nlifetime becomes \
prolonged (>> 1 week) at low temperatures. The manipulation of\\na delicate \
balance between thermal agitation and the topological stability\\nenables a \
deterministic creation/annihilation of the metastable SkL by\\nexploiting \
electric heating and subsequent rapid cooling, thus establishing a\\nfacile \
method to control the formation of a SkL.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
