            "id" -> "1808.07995", "submitter" -> "Changwei Wu", "authors" -> 
             "Chang-Wei Wu, Jun-Han Huang, Dao-Xin Yao", "title" -> 
             "Tunable room-temperature ferromagnetism in the SiC monolayer", 
             "comments" -> Null, "journal-ref" -> 
             "J. Magn. Magn. Mater. 469, 306 (2019)", "doi" -> 
             "10.1016/j.jmmm.2018.08.054", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "cond-mat.mes-hall", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  It is essential to explore two-dimensional (2D) material with \
magnetic\nordering in new generation spintronic devices. Particularly, the \
seeking of\nroom-temperature 2D ferromagnetic (FM) materials is a hot topic \
of current\nresearch. Here, we study magnetism of the Mn-doped and \
electron-doped SiC\nmonolayer using first-principle calculations. For the \
Mn-doped SiC monolayer,\nwe find that either electron or hole could mediate \
the ferromagnetism in the\nsystem and the Curie temperature ($T_C$) can be \
improved by appropriate carrier\ndoping. The codoping strategy is also \
discussed on improving $T_C$. The\ntransition between antiferromagnetic and \
FM phase can be found by strain\nengineering. The $T_C$ is improved above \
room temperature (RT) under the strain\nlarger than $0.06$. Moreover, the \
Mn-doped SiC monolayer develops half-metal at\nthe strain range of \
$0.05-0.1$. On the other hand, the direct electron doping\ncan induce \
ferromagnetism due to the van Hove singularity in density of states\nof the \
conduction band edge of the SiC monolayer. The $T_C$ is found to be\naround \
RT. These fascinating controllable electronic and magnetic properties\nare \
desired for spintronic applications.\n", 
