physics.optics\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  The optical properties of C$_{60}$ single crystals, \
intercalated with\\nnitrogen molecules, were investigated by the \
spectral-luminescence method in\\nthe temperature range 20 - 230 K. The \
saturation was carried out under the\\npressure of 30 atm. of N$_{2}$ at \
various temperatures ranging from 200 to\\n550$^{\\\\circ}$C. For the \
C$_{60}+$N$_{2}$ system, the presence of a temperature\\nboundary of the \
adsorption crossover of about 420$^{\\\\circ}$C was established\\n(transition \
from the diffusion mechanism of intercalation - physisorption, to\\nchemical \
interaction - chemisorption). The temperature dependence of the\\nintegrated \
radiation intensity of a new nitrogen-containing substance based on\\nthe \
C$_{60}$ fullerite has been recorded for the first time. Quenching \
of\\nphotoluminescence at low temperatures was found. The observed new effect \
of\\nlow-temperature quenching of photoluminescence is explained by the \
appearance\\nin the process of chemical interaction between the N$_{2}$ \
impurity molecules\\nand the C$_{60}$ matrix of effective exciton trapping \
centers and nonradiative\\ndeactivation of electronic excitation.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
