                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Spacetime symmetries and the qubit Bloch ball: a \
physical derivation of\\n  finite dimensional quantum theory and the number \
of spatial dimensions\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Accepted manuscript. To appear in Physical Review \
A\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Phys. Rev. A 104, 032220 (2021)\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevA.104.032220\""}]}, {
               
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"quant-ph gr-qc\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  Quantum theory and relativity are the pillar theories \
on which our\\nunderstanding of physics is based. Poincar\\\\'e invariance is \
a fundamental\\nphysical principle stating that the experimental results must \
be the same in\\nall inertial reference frames in Minkowski spacetime. It is \
a basic condition\\nimposed on quantum theory in order to construct quantum \
field theories, hence,\\nit plays a fundamental role in the standard model of \
particle physics too. As\\nis well known, Minkowski spacetime follows from \
clear physical principles, like\\nthe relativity principle and the invariance \
of the speed of light. Here, we\\nreproduce such a derivation, but leave the \
number of spatial dimensions $n$ as\\na free variable. Then, assuming that \
spacetime is Minkowski in $1+n$ dimensions\\nand within the framework of \
general probabilistic theories, we reconstruct the\\nqubit Bloch ball and \
finite dimensional quantum theory, and obtain that the\\nnumber of spatial \
dimensions must be $n=3$, from Poincar\\\\'e invariance and\\nother physical \
postulates. Our results suggest a fundamental physical\\nconnection between \
spacetime and quantum theory.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
