             "abstract" -> 
             "  Microwave delivery to samples in a cryogenic environment can \
pose\nexperimental challenges such as restricting optical access, space \
constraints\nand heat generation. Moreover, existing solutions that overcome \
various\nexperimental restrictions do not necessarily provide a large, \
homogeneous\noscillating magnetic field over macroscopic lengthscales, which \
is required for\ncontrol of spin ensembles or fast gate operations in \
scaled-up quantum\ncomputing implementations. Here we show fast and coherent \
control of a\nnegatively charged nitrogen vacancy spin ensemble by taking \
advantage of the\nhigh permittivity of a KTaO3 dielectric resonator at \
cryogenic temperatures. We\nachieve Rabi frequencies of up to 48 MHz, with \
the total field-to-power\nconversion ratio $C_{\\rm P} = $ 9.66 \
mT/$\\sqrt{\\rm W}$ ($\\approx191$\nMHz/$\\sqrt{\\rm W}$). We use the \
nitrogen vacancy center spin ensemble to probe\nthe quality factor, the \
coherent enhancement, and the spatial distribution of\nthe magnetic field \
inside the diamond sample. The key advantages of the\ndielectric resonator \
utilised in this work are: ease of assembly, in-situ\ntuneability, a high \
magnetic field conversion efficiency, a low volume\nfootprint, and optical \
transparency. This makes KTaO3 dielectric resonators a\npromising platform \
for the delivery of microwave fields for the control of\nspins in various \
materials at cryogenic temperatures.\n", 
