signal strength\nand annotation cost of training data. For example, machine \
translation data\nrange from costly expert-generated translations that enable \
supervised\nlearning, to weak quality-judgment feedback that facilitate \
reinforcement\nlearning. We present the first user study on annotation cost \
and machine\nlearnability for the less popular annotation mode of error \
markings. We show\nthat error markings for translations of TED talks from \
English to German allow\nprecise credit assignment while requiring \
significantly less human effort than\ncorrecting/post-editing, and that \
error-marked data can be used successfully to\nfine-tune neural machine \
translation models.\n", 
