             "abstract" -> 
             "  Gaze following estimates gaze targets of in-scene person by \
understanding\nhuman behavior and scene information. Existing methods usually \
analyze scene\nimages for gaze following. However, compared with visual \
images, audio also\nprovides crucial cues for determining human behavior.This \
suggests that we can\nfurther improve gaze following considering audio cues. \
In this paper, we\nexplore gaze following tasks in conversational scenarios. \
We propose a novel\nmulti-modal gaze following framework based on our \
observation ``audiences tend\nto focus on the speaker''. We first leverage \
the correlation between audio and\nlips, and classify speakers and listeners \
in a scene. We then use the identity\ninformation to enhance scene images and \
propose a gaze candidate estimation\nnetwork. The network estimates gaze \
candidates from enhanced scene images and\nwe use MLP to match subjects with \
candidates as classification tasks. Existing\ngaze following datasets focus \
on visual images while ignore audios.To evaluate\nour method, we collect a \
conversational dataset, VideoGazeSpeech (VGS), which\nis the first gaze \
following dataset including images and audio. Our method\nsignificantly \
outperforms existing methods in VGS datasets. The visualization\nresult also \
prove the advantage of audio cues in gaze following tasks. Our work\nwill \
inspire more researches in multi-modal gaze following estimation.\n", 
