            "id" -> "2207.05473", "submitter" -> "Alberto Testolin Dr.", 
             "authors" -> "Matteo Zambra, Alberto Testolin, Marco Zorzi", 
             "title" -> 
             "A developmental approach for training deep belief networks", 
             "comments" -> Null, "journal-ref" -> 
             "Cognitive Computation, 2022", "doi" -> 
             "10.1007/s12559-022-10085-5", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "cs.LG cs.NE", "license" -> 
             "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/", "abstract" -> 
             "  Deep belief networks (DBNs) are stochastic neural networks \
that can extract\nrich internal representations of the environment from the \
sensory data. DBNs\nhad a catalytic effect in triggering the deep learning \
revolution,\ndemonstrating for the very first time the feasibility of \
unsupervised learning\nin networks with many layers of hidden neurons. These \
hierarchical\narchitectures incorporate plausible biological and cognitive \
properties, making\nthem particularly appealing as computational models of \
human perception and\ncognition. However, learning in DBNs is usually carried \
out in a greedy,\nlayer-wise fashion, which does not allow to simulate the \
holistic maturation of\ncortical circuits and prevents from modeling \
cognitive development. Here we\npresent iDBN, an iterative learning algorithm \
for DBNs that allows to jointly\nupdate the connection weights across all \
layers of the model. We evaluate the\nproposed iterative algorithm on two \
different sets of visual stimuli, measuring\nthe generative capabilities of \
the learned model and its potential to support\nsupervised downstream tasks. \
We also track network development in terms of\ngraph theoretical properties \
and investigate the potential extension of iDBN to\ncontinual learning \
scenarios. DBNs trained using our iterative approach achieve\na final \
performance comparable to that of the greedy counterparts, at the same\ntime \
allowing to accurately analyze the gradual development of internal\n\
representations in the deep network and the progressive improvement in task\n\
performance. Our work paves the way to the use of iDBN for modeling\n\
neurocognitive development.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
                "Tue, 12 Jul 2022 11:37:58 GMT"}, {
               "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
                "Tue, 2 Aug 2022 14:10:14 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
             "2024-02-08", 
