                 "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1038/s41467-023-36657-z\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"cs.LG cond-mat.dis-nn cond-mat.stat-mech physics.comp-ph \
physics.data-an\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  Given a finite and noisy dataset generated with a \
closed-form mathematical\\nmodel, when is it possible to learn the true \
generating model from the data\\nalone? This is the question we investigate \
here. We show that this\\nmodel-learning problem displays a transition from a \
low-noise phase in which\\nthe true model can be learned, to a phase in which \
the observation noise is too\\nhigh for the true model to be learned by any \
method. Both in the low-noise\\nphase and in the high-noise phase, \
probabilistic model selection leads to\\noptimal generalization to unseen \
data. This is in contrast to standard machine\\nlearning approaches, \
including artificial neural networks, which in this\\nparticular problem are \
limited, in the low-noise phase, by their ability to\\ninterpolate. In the \
transition region between the learnable and unlearnable\\nphases, \
generalization is hard for all approaches including probabilistic \
model\\nselection.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
