                 "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.101.124045\""}]}, {
               
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", "\"gr-qc\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  We present the $d+1$ formulation of \
Einstein-scalar-Gauss-Bonnet (ESGB)\\ntheories in dimension $D=d+1$ and for \
arbitrary (spacelike or timelike)\\nslicings. We first build an action which \
generalizes those of\\nGibbons-Hawking-York and Myers to ESGB theories, \
showing that they can be\\ndescribed by a Dirichlet variational principle. We \
then generalize the\\nArnowitt-Deser-Misner (ADM) Lagrangian and Hamiltonian \
to ESGB theories, as\\nwell as the resulting $d+1$ decomposition of the \
equations of motion. Unlike\\ngeneral relativity, the canonical momenta of \
ESGB theories are nonlinear in the\\nextrinsic curvature. This has two main \
implications: (i) the ADM Hamiltonian is\\ngenerically multivalued, and the \
associated Hamiltonian evolution is not\\npredictable; (ii) the \\\"$d+1$\\\" \
equations of motion are quasilinear, and they may\\nbreak down in strongly \
curved, highly dynamical regimes. Our results should be\\nuseful to guide \
future developments of numerical relativity for ESGB gravity in\\nthe \
nonperturbative regime.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
